{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction Law criminal-conviction eviction grounds","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of, or found civilly liable for, assault, threats, theft, drug activity on the premises, or human trafficking. A three-day notice to quit is required, and suit must be filed within two years.","key_value":"3-day notice to quit; 2-year filing limit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of residential premises who are convicted of, plead guilty to, or are found civilly liable for the listed offenses, or who house such persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential rental premises"},"exemptions":"No eviction where a tenant allows a juvenile found delinquent for drug use or possession to reside at the property.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may file for eviction, if the tenant has been convicted of or pleaded guilty to, or if a juvenile has been found to be delinquent by the Court due to an offense involving theft of property from the landlord or from tenants residing in the same building or complex.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Owner-occupied two- and three-family dwelling eviction causes","requirement":"A tenant of a landlord-occupied two- or three-family dwelling may be removed only by court order and only for listed causes such as holding over, nonpayment, disorderly conduct, damage or rule violations. Notice is three months for at-will or year-to-year tenancies, one month for month-to-month.","key_value":"3-month notice (at-will/year-to-year); 1-month (month-to-month)","coverage_conditions":"Landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings; notice periods vary by tenancy type.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"landlord-occupied two- or three-family dwelling"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction. However, in these cases, the landlord must prove that the tenant (a) is staying after the expiration of the term of the lease, (b) is staying after a failure to pay rent,","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for Eviction Law violations","requirement":"A landlord who evicts to personally occupy a unit must occupy it at least six months, or the former tenant may recover triple damages. Demolition or board-up evictions bar residential use for five years, with a civil penalty up to $10,000 per violation.","key_value":"6-month occupancy; 5-year residential-use bar; treble damages; up to $10,000 civil penalty","coverage_conditions":"Landlords who obtained vacancy by notice of personal occupancy, permanent board-up, demolition or permanent retirement from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000 per violation; property may not be used residentially or registered as a planned real estate development for five years.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months. If instead the landlord permits personal occupancy of the unit by another tenant or registration of conversion of the property to a condominium or cooperative, the landlord is liable to the former tenant for three (3) times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal against tenants exercising civil rights","requirement":"A landlord may not retaliate by eviction, lease alteration or refusal to renew when a tenant enforces lease or legal rights, makes a good-faith complaint to a government authority after written notice to the landlord, joins a tenant organization, or refuses a retaliatory lease change.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"all residential rental properties including mobile homes"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10). The law against reprisal applies to all rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure eviction protections for tenants","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction, and the residential tenant stays with the property when a lender or buyer takes possession. A buyer who will personally occupy the home must give the tenant a 90-day notice to quit.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants of foreclosed properties; month-to-month tenants and owner-occupied two- and three-family units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"foreclosed residential rental property"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90-day notice to quit when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy","requirement":"Tenants at least 62 years old, permanently disabled, or certain disabled veterans who lived in a building being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership for at least one year may be protected from eviction for 40 years, subject to income limits.","key_value":"40 years' protected tenancy","coverage_conditions":"Tenants 62 or older, permanently disabled, or disabled veterans (60% or more service-connected), resident at least one year before the conversion recording date; income limit of 3 times county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"unspecified","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"senior and disabled tenants in buildings being converted; one-year residency before conversion recording"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 protected tenancy","requirement":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 extends protected tenancy to qualified tenants in qualified counties (currently only Hudson County) whose buildings are being converted, if they were not eligible under the Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties (only Hudson County) in buildings converted or being converted, not eligible under the 1981 Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"converted buildings in qualified counties (Hudson County)"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction requirements","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless a Rooming and Boarding House Act exception applies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"rooming and boarding houses (unrelated occupants, no private kitchens or baths)"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
